artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a heterostructure composed of two stacked two-dimensional van der Waals layers: a ferromagnetic (FM) layer with ordered spin moments and an antiferromagnetic (AFM) layer with compensated/uncompensated sublattice moments, joined across an atomically sharp interface held together only by van der Waals forces. After field-cooling the system below the Néel temperature of the AFM layer, the hysteresis loop of the FM layer is observed to be shifted along the magnetic-field axis (the exchange-bias phenomenon).

Problem. Identify and characterize the exact microscopic spin–spin coupling mechanism acting between the FM spins and the AFM spins across the interface that is responsible for the observed exchange bias in such two-dimensional van der Waals FM/AFM heterostructures. In particular, determine:
(a) the dominant interfacial exchange Hamiltonian (e.g. Heisenberg direct exchange, superexchange through intervening ligands, Dzyaloshinskii–Moriya interaction, RKKY-type coupling, or proximity-induced coupling), including its symmetry and sign;
(b) the role, density, and configuration of uncompensated interfacial AFM moments;
(c) how interfacial roughness, stacking order, twist angle, and van der Waals gap distance modulate this coupling; and
(d) a quantitative prediction of the exchange-bias field as a function of the parameters in (a)–(c) that can be compared with experiment.
